---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import { SITE, BOOKING_URL } from "../consts";

import HeroSection from "../components/sections/workshop/HeroSection.astro";
import ProblemStatementSection from "../components/sections/workshop/ProblemStatementSection.astro";
import OnTheDaySection from "../components/sections/workshop/OnTheDaySection.astro";
import LeaveWithSection from "../components/sections/workshop/LeaveWithSection.astro";
import WhatItCostsSection from "../components/sections/workshop/WhatItCostsSection.astro";
import FaqSection from "../components/sections/workshop/FaqSection.astro";
import CloseSection from "../components/sections/workshop/CloseSection.astro";

/*
 * /workshop — the AI Opportunity Workshop. Built Aug 26 2026 against the approved copy
 * in docs/workshop-page.md. It had been a footer link to a 404 since the site shipped.
 *
 * REWORKED THE SAME DAY against a replaced copy file. The first version described the
 * workshop; this one argues for it. Section 2 was rewritten end to end — it used to say
 * who the workshop is for, and now it says why the first decision is the one that costs,
 * which is a different and much harder job. The hero, the close heading, the meta
 * description and one FAQ answer changed with it. See CLAUDE.md for what moved and why.
 *
 * WHO ARRIVES HERE: someone who has read another ArkSystems page, agrees with it, and
 * cannot name a single problem to start with. They have several candidates and no way to
 * rank them. The page has to make choosing the workshop feel like the sensible move
 * rather than an admission that they don't know their own business — which is why not one
 * line on it implies the reader is disorganised or behind. The standing rule holds here
 * as everywhere: blame the work, never the owner.
 *
 * THE FIRST JOB, BEFORE ANY OTHER, is establishing that identifying the right problem is
 * itself the valuable work. Until that lands, a workshop reads as a fee charged before
 * the real work starts. Once it lands, the workshop IS the work — the decision every
 * dollar spent afterwards depends on. Section 2 does that job and nothing else does.
 *
 * THE SECOND JOB is answering the question the free call creates: why pay for this when
 * the call costs nothing? The answer, in section 6 and again in section 2 and again in
 * the close, is that they do different jobs. Discovery examines one problem already
 * identified. The workshop is for when nothing has been identified, or when there are
 * several candidates and no basis to rank them.
 *
 * ORDER MATTERS, AND THE COPY FILE MAKES IT A BUILD NOTE: the why comes before the what.
 * Section 2 argues the value of the decision; section 3 describes the day. Reversing them
 * turns the page into an agenda — a schedule offered to someone who has not yet accepted
 * that the day is worth buying.
 *
 * THE PAGE ARGUES AGAINST ITS OWN SALE IN FOUR PLACES, and this is the thing most likely
 * to be tidied away by a future edit. Section 2's closing concession ("if you can already
 * name the one thing you'd fix, you don't need a workshop"), the first FAQ answer, the
 * second FAQ answer ("some businesses can, and should… use it"), and the close. That
 * concession is the evidence for the claim the page is actually making — that the ranking
 * we produce is honest. Remove it and the page is a brochure.
 *
 * Seven sections, in the copy file's order:
 *
 *   1  Hero                the thesis: the expensive decision is which problem, not which tool
 *   2  The problem statement  THE ARGUMENT. Why the first decision is the one that costs. No CTA
 *   3  What happens on the day  the method
 *   4  What you leave with  the deliverable. No CTA
 *   5  What it costs       the only price section. "From" is load-bearing
 *   6  Before you book     the objections
 *   7  Close               the ask
 *
 * FOUR CTAs, all pointing at BOOKING_URL: hero, section 3, section 5, close. Labelled
 * ws-hero / ws-day / ws-cost / ws-closing so this page's numbers never merge with the
 * other two. Sections 2, 4 and 6 carry none, deliberately — 2 and 4 do recognition and
 * delivery work, and 6 exists to remove objections rather than to ask.
 *
 * EVERY BUTTON BOOKS THE FREE CALL, NOT THE WORKSHOP. The Cal.com event behind
 * BOOKING_URL is the 20-minute discovery call. A button labelled "Book the workshop"
 * would be false as well as off-message.
 *
 * "AI BUDGETS GO TO THE WRONG PLACE" IN SECTION 2 IS A POSITION, NOT A CLAIM, and the
 * copy file's claims section names it. It carries no figure and no attribution, which is
 * what keeps it inside the claims rules. DO NOT ADD A STATISTIC TO IT — no percentage, no
 * survey, no "studies show". The moment it acquires a number it becomes a claim needing a
 * source, and there isn't one.
 *
 * ONE EYEBROW, on the hero. The copy file specifies it there and nowhere else, and every
 * other section's heading is already its own short name — an eyebrow above "Before you
 * book" sets the phrase twice and has a screen reader announce it twice.
 *
 * NOT LINKED FROM THE HOMEPAGE OR /accounting, by Fredrick's instruction of Aug 26 2026.
 * The copy file asks for links from the homepage's method section and from every
 * industry page's offer block. Neither is built. /accounting in particular stopped
 * selling the workshop on Aug 25 — section 6 there is one paragraph and one button, and
 * what gets recommended is decided on the call. Nav and footer carry this page for now.
 *
 * TWO ILLUSTRATIONS, and they are a pair: the hero draws the reader's position (five
 * candidates, identical rows, nothing to choose between them) and section 4 draws the
 * same five after the day (ranked, weighted, one ruled out). Neither carries a single
 * figure — there are no delivered workshops to draw numbers from, and a number in an
 * illustration is a claim whatever the copy around it says. Neither carries the gold
 * "Working system · Sample data" badge either; that badge means a system that genuinely
 * runs, and keeping it meaning one thing is what keeps it worth anything.
 *
 * Schema: Service and FAQPage, as the copy file specifies. FAQPage is emitted by the FAQ
 * component, next to the questions it describes, so the two cannot drift apart.
 *
 * LocalBusiness is NOT emitted here either, for the same reason as on /accounting: it
 * needs a real street address and real hours, and inventing either would be a false claim
 * on a page whose entire argument is that we do not do that.
 *
 * FIVE THINGS THE COPY FILE FLAGS FOR CONFIRMATION BEFORE THIS PAGE SHIPS — they are in
 * its own [CONFIRM] section, they are Fredrick's to answer, and two of them are real
 * exposure rather than tidying:
 *
 *   1. THE DELIVERABLE. Section 4 states the document carries an indicative figure per
 *      candidate. That is the strongest reason to pay for a workshop rather than wait for
 *      free discovery, and it is only true if that is genuinely what gets produced.
 *   2. THE CREDIT WINDOW. Section 5 states the credit with no expiry. An open-ended
 *      credit is a liability that never closes. If a window applies it belongs in the
 *      proposal, not on the page.
 *   3. DELIVERY TIMING. FAQ six says "shortly after the day". Honest as written because
 *      it promises nothing specific. Replace it once a real commitment exists — and until
 *      then do not invent a number, because a stated turnaround is a delivery timeline.
 *   4. WHETHER THE PRICE VARIES BY SCOPE. Section 5 says the fee follows scope. If it is
 *      really a flat CAD $1,000 with "from" as cover, that section is inaccurate.
 *   5. WHAT COUNTS AS ONE AREA. Section 3's scope callout is the boundary that protects
 *      the fee. Without an internal definition, a client can reasonably expect the day to
 *      cover everything they mention.
 */

const serviceSchema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "Service",
  name: "AI Opportunity Workshop",
  serviceType: "AI opportunity assessment workshop",
  description:
    "A day spent on one agreed area of a business, establishing which problem is worth pointing AI at: mapping how the work actually runs, putting figures against each candidate, separating the AI problems from the process problems, and producing a ranked shortlist with a written recommendation on what to build first — and what to build not at all.",
  provider: {
    "@type": "Organization",
    name: SITE.name,
    url: SITE.url,
  },
  areaServed: {
    "@type": "AdministrativeArea",
    name: "Canada",
  },
  url: `${SITE.url}/workshop`,
  potentialAction: {
    "@type": "ReserveAction",
    name: "Book your free 20-minute call",
    target: BOOKING_URL,
  },
};
---

{/*
  Title and description are the approved strings from docs/workshop-page.md, used
  character for character — the file counted them (52 and 146) and the pipe separator is
  part of what it counted, which is why this goes through titleOverride rather than being
  rebuilt from the site's em-dash pattern.

  The title carries "Vancouver", which is now the third and last place on the site that
  names the location. Fredrick's decision of Aug 26 2026, taken on the same reasoning as
  the two existing ones: a title tag is a search-targeting string rather than a sentence
  on a page, and the copy file's target queries are local. No sentence anywhere on this
  page names a place.
*/}
<BaseLayout
  title="AI Opportunity Workshop"
  titleOverride="AI Opportunity Workshop | ArkSystems Vancouver"
  description="Not sure where AI fits your business? One day on one area of your operation, ending with a ranked shortlist and what to build first. From CAD $1,000."
>
  <script
    slot="head"
    type="application/ld+json"
    set:html={JSON.stringify(serviceSchema)}
    is:inline
  />

  <HeroSection />
  <ProblemStatementSection />
  <OnTheDaySection />
  <LeaveWithSection />
  <WhatItCostsSection />
  <FaqSection />
  <CloseSection />
</BaseLayout>
